=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** iptables/nftables FORWARD chain on router6, applied to its WAN-facing interface `eth2` (10.50.1.2/24 — the link to router3 and the rest of the network).

**Mechanism:** Router6 carries three chain rules that together drop all transit traffic arriving from eth2: (1) ACCEPT TCP from 172.16.10.0/24 destined for 172.18.51.1:80 only; (2) DROP all other TCP from eth2; (3) DROP all UDP from eth2; (4) DROP everything else from eth2 (including ICMP — 30 packets already counted by the counter). Because the FORWARD chain is only applied to transit (forwarded) packets, router6's own locally-originated pings still work, which created the misleading appearance of a one-way asymmetry (router6 → pc11 succeeds; pc11 → pc17 fails). All traffic from any external subnet (192.168.200.x, 172.16.10.x, any router intermediate hop) trying to reach 172.18.51.x or 172.18.50.x through router6 is silently dropped.

**Localization:** router6, `eth2` ingress rules in both the legacy iptables FORWARD chain and the nftables `ip filter` FORWARD chain. The same rules appear duplicated in both frameworks, which is why they took effect regardless of which netfilter path was active.

**Root cause:** An overly restrictive ACL was applied to router6's WAN interface (eth2), dropping all forwarded traffic except one narrow TCP/80 exception, blocking all ICMP, UDP, and general TCP transit from the wider network into the 172.18.51.x/172.18.50.x site — not a router4 crash.

**Fix:** Remove the DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain on eth2 (or replace with appropriate permit rules). In nftables: `nft flush chain ip filter FORWARD`; in iptables: `iptables -F FORWARD`.
=== END ===